🐛 Describe the bug
When a custom device backend relies on CPU fallback for unimplemented operations, CompositeExplicitAutograd operations fail to return properly sized tensors. This affects operations like abs, isfinite, isinf, and isnan.
assuming we have implemented the minimal set of ops as described: https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/main/accelerator/operators.html for a privateuse1 out-of-tree backend, and implementing the cpu fallback as described (just a call to at::native::cpu_fallback(op, stack);)
# This fails - returns tensor with shape [0]
t = torch.randn(4, 4, device='privateuse1')
result = torch.abs(t)  # result.shape == torch.Size([0])

# But these work correctly
torch.abs(t, out=pre_allocated_tensor)  # Works
t.abs_()  # Works (in-place)
torch.neg(t)  # Works (uses structured_delegate)
Possible Root Cause
1. Implementation Pattern
In pytorch/aten/src/ATen/native/UnaryOps.cpp:
// Line 471-480
template <typename OutImpl>
static inline Tensor unary_op_impl_with_complex_to_float(const Tensor& self, OutImpl& out_impl) {
  if (self.is_complex()) {
    const auto float_type = c10::toRealValueType(self.scalar_type());
    Tensor result = at::empty_like(self, self.options().dtype(float_type));
    return out_impl(result, self);
  }

  Tensor result = at::empty({0}, self.options());  // Creates placeholder with shape [0]
  return out_impl(result, self);  // Expects out_impl to resize
}

// Line 546-548
Tensor abs(const Tensor& self) {
  return unary_op_impl_with_complex_to_float(self, at::abs_out);
}
The pattern creates an empty tensor with shape [0] as a placeholder, expecting the .out variant (at::abs_out) to resize it appropriately.
2. How the Resize is Expected to Happen
When out_impl(result, self) is called with the empty [0] shaped tensor, here's the expected resize chain:


at::abs_out() is called (UnaryOps.cpp:543-544):
Tensor& abs_out(const Tensor& self, Tensor& result) {
  return unary_op_impl_with_complex_to_float_out(result, self, abs_stub, false);
}


Creates TensorIterator (UnaryOps.cpp:405-408):
Tensor& unary_op_impl_out(Tensor& result, const Tensor& self, Stub& stub) {
  auto iter = TensorIterator::unary_op(result, self);  // KEY: This should handle resize
  stub(iter.device_type(), iter);
  return result;
}


TensorIterator detects empty tensor and marks for resize (TensorIterator.cpp:1203-1210):
if (!output.defined()) {
  operands_[i].will_resize = true;
}
if (output.defined() && !output.sizes().equals(shape_)) {
  if (config.resize_outputs_ && !operands_[i].is_read_write) {
    operands_[i].will_resize = true;  // Marks empty [0] tensor for resize
  }
}


TensorIterator allocates/resizes output (TensorIterator.cpp:574-601):
// In allocate_or_resize_outputs()
if (!op.tensor_base().defined() || op.will_resize) {
  // Calculates correct shape and strides
  set_output_raw_strided(i, tensor_shape, tensor_stride, original_options(op), names_);
}


Actual resize happens (TensorIterator.cpp:1644-1651):
if (!op.tensor_base().defined()) {
  // Creates new tensor with correct shape
  op.tensor(c10::MaybeOwned<TensorBase>::owned(at::empty(sizes, options)));
} else if (op.will_resize) {
  // Resizes existing tensor to correct shape
  at::native::resize_output(op.tensor(), sizes);
}


3. Why the Resize is Lost
When the operation goes through CPU fallback (pytorch/aten/src/ATen/native/CPUFallback.cpp):


Input Conversion (lines 114-147):
// Simplified from CPUFallback.cpp
auto ivalue = (*stack)[idx];
if (ivalue.isTensor()) {
  auto src_tensor = ivalue.toTensor();
  // Convert to CPU tensor
  (*stack)[idx] = c10::IValue(src_tensor.cpu());
}

Custom device tensors are converted to CPU tensors
The empty [0] shaped result tensor passed as input is also converted to CPU
At this point, stack contains CPU versions of all tensors



CPU Operation Execution (line 150):
op.redispatchBoxed(c10::DispatchKeySet(cpu_dispatch_key), stack);

The CPU implementation of abs_out is called with the CPU tensors
The CPU abs_out implementation:

Receives the [0] shaped CPU tensor
Creates TensorIterator which marks it for resize
Resizes the CPU tensor to the correct shape
Fills it with computed values


Critical: The resize happens to the CPU tensor on the stack, not the original device tensor



Output Conversion (lines 194-341):
// Process return values
for (const auto idx : c10::irange(returns.size())) {
  const AliasInfo* alias_info = schema_returns[idx].alias_info();

  if (alias_info != nullptr && alias_info->isWrite()) {
    // For mutable operations (like .out variants with alias info)
    // Uses _copy_from_and_resize to handle size changes
    at::_copy_from_and_resize(cpu_tensor, original_device_tensor);
  } else {
    // For new tensor returns (no alias info)
    // Line 321-324:
    if (returns[idx].isTensor() && returns[idx].toTensor().defined()) {
      (*stack)[returns_begin + idx] =
          c10::IValue(returns[idx].toTensor().to(*tgt_device));
    }
  }
}


The Core Problem:

For abs(tensor) (no alias info), the code path goes through the else branch
returns[idx].toTensor() retrieves the tensor from the stack position
But for CompositeExplicitAutograd operations:

The original stack had a [0] shaped placeholder device tensor
This was converted to a [0] shaped CPU tensor
The CPU operation resized this CPU tensor on the stack
But when converting back, the code uses .to(*tgt_device) on the CPU tensor
The .to() operation creates a new device tensor with the same shape as the CPU tensor
However, the issue is that the return value extraction logic doesn't account for the fact that the original device tensor placeholder is still [0] shaped





Why This Breaks:
The fundamental assumption in the CPU fallback is that operations either:

Modify existing tensors (have alias info) → handled via _copy_from_and_resize
Return completely new tensors → assumed to be properly sized already

But CompositeExplicitAutograd operations with the [0] placeholder pattern create a third case:

They pass a placeholder that needs resizing
The resize happens during the operation
But there's no mechanism to propagate this resize back to the device tensor



